e families facing and  then David went off and wrote this  fantastic screenplay at the same time  I've got Marc Shaiman and Scott Whitman  at the table with Rob writing these  credible songs these marvelous lyrics  again with such a love and passion for  the original but making it their own and  then mark scoring the film beyond the  song so brilliantly and so it for as a  Purdue to and then a magnificent team of  designers like John and and Sandeep alva  costumes and Dionne bee-bees marvelous  photography so there's a producer I know  that was very long-winded answer but  this is the joy this has been the joy of  my life it starts with this man these  glorious actors and all those actors you  saw and it comes at a time where we all  need it so badly to remember possibility  and and the fact that not only do we  want to remember our childhood we  actually need to now so that's my  experience on Mary Bob what was the  hardest task of having the return in  this news story and at the same time  pain it's creating the world that felt  like it belonged to what have been  created by the Sherman Brothers asking  what was the hardest part of it yeah  well what was the easiest part the  Sherman Brothers are right there and  then  oh we're gonna also write songs for  lin-manuel Miranda so I got to the piano  [Laughter]  that would be the hardest part  everything else and so Meryl Streep and  Angela Lansbury and Emily Blunt no  problem no what was going through your  head as all of this came shooting down  the pike to you to make real  well first off how fun is all that I  just always say I have the best job at  the entire world and I think everyone  here feels they have the best job in the  entire world and this movie was possibly  our best mark and I tease each other  whenever we work together and say this  is our best one yet mark always grumbles  but last night he came to me and he said  you know what John this is the best one  yeah I had a great introduction to this  because and we haven't really talked  about this but maybe 9 10 years ago when  we were doing 9 we started talking about  Mary Poppins and you let me in on this  wonderful story where you knew Julie  Andrews and Tony Walton and they told  you Tony Walton told you where he got  inspiration for some of the sets from  the first movie and so I went on a hunt  I had my little notes from you  and I went to all the parks trying to  find houses that look like boats and and  found the boltons crescents so I had  been waiting for the phone call from you  for 10 years we actually designed the  ride well we were location scouting on a  movie yeah so so it was it was kind of  deep in all of our Bloods yeah so Emily  I want to ask you I from watching the  film I think everybody can actually feel  it when Mary Poppins comes floating down  which must have been a fun shot I don't  know exactly how you did that but not  frightening at all  yeah I had the best seat in the house  for that one I mean not that I could see  Lynn because Lynn was like that big you  know that was a practical shot she's 60  feet up on a line coming down I thought  I was alright with Heights until I was  up there and then I realized I wasn't  you know it's just and I just remember  being up there and thinking and that  really was the moment that entrance  where Rob very helpfully and in a very  emboldened  lead the incredible score blaring from  the speakers and so it did help me sort  of get over my yeah we probably messed  up the shot over at solo which was  shooting on the same but it was that  moment of come and it was all in one  shot so I start this big in the air and  I come down and I have to walk into the  shot and start speaking and so but there  was that moment up there where I was  like oh my god I'm Mary Poppins and  Meryl Streep when she saw that maybe she  goes how much you pay Rob Marshall for  that entrance response talk about  producing the music for this and making  this very lush I think soundtrack and  and making this whole thing seamless  well something Emily said reminding me  now this is where Rob Marshall is some  kind of mad genius because before we  filmed he asked me to write a whole  bunch of score and I was like you're  crazy how can I score nothing he said  well this has got to be this and it's  the sequence in this and I want to have  this music on the set so I went back to  New York and in our hiatus or Christmas  hiatus and I read the script and then I  actually read the script like a radio  play including all the stage directions  and I I tried to read it in the pace so  I imagined it might be in the movie but  you know things change when you film a  movie so I was like you know scoring is  so specific how can I do this but I just  finally just trusted he he had he had  trust and we recorded all this score  before they filmed so that they did have  music when they were filming and I'll be  damned if every single piece of music  that we filmed recorded before they  filmed is all still in the movie like  like bespoke I mean we we barely made  any adjustments on those specific cues  because he just he has it in his head  and and then he puts it up there and I'd  never experienced anything like that  yeah like when we're riding to Topsy's  house and it's like done and sort of  like the pre reprise of Topsy song and  serve this jazzy score we were blasting  that on the streets of London and the  whole tempo every every actor who's  walking by as we're going is is going to  that tempo it really does set the mood  for for everybody and it's it's like  what what better way to sort of put us  all in a pop in space especially if  we're in a practical location to pick up  on that because mark makes it sound so  easy but I have to go back just again to  Rob because the his movies seemed so  seamless and so effortless directing an  original musical and one this  complicated for reasons you're now  getting a glimpse of starting with the  amount of artists the amount of crafts  women and craftsmen who flocked to work  with Rob directing an original musical  is really really challenging and  sophisticated not just the songs and the  score the things that are obvious  there's a musicality that's built into  the way the camera moves the way the  lighting is the way transitions from  scene to scene if you look at that  animated sequence alone think about it  start it with an idea in Rob's head it  becomes a song and lyric it gets  arranged it gets orchestrated it is lit  it has an whole animation team under  Rob's Direction inventing the story  inventing this world if you will he's  got the best actors in the world doing  their best work and then it's all  choreographed there are penguins and  life people doing he does the  choreography with his partner John it  notes like the Penguins a little heavier  on the cane when you pull him it's  really and I say that just because I  because it's all true and as I said in  the beginning you're so lucky to get to  work with Rob I've sat on the stage  before with some marvelous directors and  in musicals as well and this this man is  really something special and you're  getting a glimpse in these little  details as to why but you make it look  so easy and the other thing I want to  say is the easiest job for a producer is  casting a Rob Marshall movie because if  reactor in this town wants to come work  with Rob and you see why it's just  beautiful so Rob thank you for all from  all of us really thank you very much  thanks everybody


